+1.1 Put $Y=\operatorname{Rep}_Q(\mathbf a,\mathbf b)$. The block description makes $Y$ an affine finite-type scheme. The block parabolic is $P_{\mathbf a,\mathbf b}=U\rtimes L$ with underlying scheme $\mathbb A^u\times L$, where $L=G_{\mathbf a}\times G_{\mathbf b}$; hence $P_{\mathbf a,\mathbf b}$ is affine and finite type, and it is smooth because $U$ is a vector group and each general linear factor of $L$ is smooth by [F5]. The quotient definition in [F1] makes $Y\to[Y/P_{\mathbf a,\mathbf b}]$ representable: after base change by a scheme $T\to[Y/P_{\mathbf a,\mathbf b}]$ corresponding to a $P_{\mathbf a,\mathbf b}$-torsor $R\to T$, the pullback is $R$. Fppf-local triviality and [F8] make this torsor an affine scheme over $T$. To see that $R\to T$ is smooth, extend its structure group to $G_{\mathbf a+\mathbf b}$; the resulting $G$-torsor is a tuple of frame bundles of vector bundles $E_i$, while the reduction to $P_{\mathbf a,\mathbf b}$ specifies subbundles $F_i\subset E_i$ of ranks $a_i$ with locally free quotients. Zariski-locally these sequences split and the bundles admit adapted frames, so $R\to T$ is locally $P_{\mathbf a,\mathbf b}\times T\to T$, which is smooth by [F9] and [F10]; it is surjective as a torsor. For two underlying $P_{\mathbf a,\mathbf b}$-torsors $R,R'$ over $T$, their equivariant isomorphism functor is fppf-locally represented by $P_{\mathbf a,\mathbf b}\times T$, and these affine local representatives descend by [F8]. Fppf-locally trivialize both torsors, with their equivariant maps given by $y,y':T'\to Y$. The compatible isomorphisms are cut out in $P_{\mathbf a,\mathbf b}\times T'$ by $g\cdot y=y'$, the inverse image of $\Delta_Y$ under $(g,t)\mapsto(g\cdot y(t),y'(t))$. This is closed because $Y$ is separated, and the closed subscheme descends effectively by [F8]. Thus the diagonal is affine representable. By [F8], $[Y/P_{\mathbf a,\mathbf b}]$ is an algebraic stack. Now, for each arrow $h:i\to j$, the free off-diagonal block is $\operatorname{Hom}(V_i^{\mathbf b},V_j^{\mathbf a})$; hence $Y\to \operatorname{Rep}_Q(\mathbf a)\times\operatorname{Rep}_Q(\mathbf b)$ is a vector bundle of rank $e=\sum_{h:i\to j}b_i a_j$. The kernel $U$ of $P_{\mathbf a,\mathbf b}\to L$ consists of upper-right vertex blocks and has dimension $u=\sum_i a_i b_i$. [F1, F5, F8, F9, F10, given, construct]
 
 1.2 Let an arbitrary scheme $T$ map to $\mathfrak M_{\mathbf a+\mathbf b}$. By the quotient-stack definition this is a family of vector bundles $E_i$ of ranks $a_i+b_i$, with arrow maps. The base change of $p$ parametrizes subbundles $F_i\subset E_i$ of ranks $a_i$ preserved by every arrow. It is the closed subscheme of the relative Grassmannian $\prod_i\operatorname{Gr}_T(a_i,E_i)$ cut out by the vanishing of the bundle maps $F_i\to E_j\to E_j/F_j$ for each arrow $h:i\to j$. The relative Plucker map sends $F_i$ to the line subbundle $\det(F_i)\subseteq\bigwedge^{a_i}E_i$. Under the quotient convention of [[def-projective-bundle-scheme]], this is represented by the quotient line of $(\bigwedge^{a_i}E_i)^\vee$ obtained by dualizing the inclusion. Thus each relative Grassmannian is a closed subscheme of $\mathbb P_T((\bigwedge^{a_i}E_i)^\vee)$; on local frames this is the closed Plucker embedding of [F3], and the equations glue under changes of frame. Each such projective bundle is proper: after any base change $T'\to T$, trivialize $E_i$ over an open cover of $T'$; on each open the projection is a projective-space projection, hence proper by [F4]. The image of a closed set is closed on each member of the cover and therefore closed globally; separatedness and finite type are also local on the base. Thus every base change is proper by [F7]. The product of these proper maps is proper by base change and composition [F4]. The arrow conditions are closed because they are zero loci of regular bundle maps, so the incidence scheme is proper over $T$ as a closed subscheme of that product [F4]. In the standard frame atlas this scheme is exactly $ I_{\mathbf a,\mathbf b}= \{(X,(F_i)):\dim F_i=a_i,\ X_h(F_i)\subseteq F_j \text{ for every }h:i\to j\}. $, and its defining equations are the same zero-locus equations just described; in an ordinary Grassmannian chart they are the vanishing of the complementary matrix block. Hence every scheme base change of $p$ is a proper scheme. [F1, F3, F4, F6, F7, construct]
 
